{
  "domain": "daily",
  "problems": [
    {
      "title": "Disk full on Docker/Dokploy hosts and CI runners",
      "area": "Disk & resources",
      "level": "L1",
      "tool": "Docker / Dokploy / GitLab Runner / Linux",
      "context": "The most common night page on container hosts. Builds, old images and unrotated container logs grow until the root filesystem fills, and then Docker, databases and runners all fail together.",
      "error_text": "write /var/lib/docker/...: no space left on device | ERROR: Job failed (system failure): ... no space left on device",
      "frequency_signal": "Long-running classic on Server Fault and Stack Overflow. Dan Luu's postmortem list has a filesystem filled by batch-job logs. GitLab documents weekly cache clearing for runners because of it.",
      "root_causes": [
        "The json-file log driver defaults to max-size -1 (unlimited), so container logs never rotate",
        "Build cache, dangling images and old releases left behind by CI and Dokploy deploys",
        "Runner caches and volumes never cleaned, and Harbor running without tag retention or garbage collection",
        "journald or syslog growth, or backups written to the same disk",
        "The only alert is a fixed 90% threshold with no trend, so it fires at 3 AM"
      ],
      "diagnosis": [
        "df -hT && df -i",
        "sudo du -xh --max-depth=1 / 2>/dev/null | sort -h | tail -15",
        "docker system df -v",
        "sudo du -xh --max-depth=1 /var/lib/docker | sort -h",
        "sudo find /var/lib/docker/containers -name '*-json.log' -size +500M -exec ls -lh {} \\;",
        "journalctl --disk-usage"
      ],
      "fix": [
        "docker builder prune -af --filter until=168h",
        "docker image prune -af --filter until=168h   # keep the images you need for rollback",
        "Emergency only: sudo truncate -s 0 /var/lib/docker/containers/ID/ID-json.log",
        "Set {\"log-driver\":\"json-file\",\"log-opts\":{\"max-size\":\"10m\",\"max-file\":\"3\"}} in /etc/docker/daemon.json, restart Docker, then recreate the containers. Existing containers keep their old log settings.",
        "sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=500M, and set SystemMaxUse=500M in /etc/systemd/journald.conf",
        "Runners: run /usr/share/gitlab-runner/clear-docker-cache weekly from cron. Harbor: add a tag retention policy and scheduled garbage collection."
      ],
      "prevention": "Alert on the trend, for example predict_linear(node_filesystem_avail_bytes{mountpoint=\"/\"}[6h], 24*3600) < 0, as well as on 80% and 90%. Put /var/lib/docker on its own volume and bake log rotation into the Ansible baseline.",
      "sources": [
        {"title": "Docker docs: JSON File logging driver (max-size defaults to unlimited)", "url": "https://docs.docker.com/engine/logging/drivers/json-file/", "type": "docs", "verified": true},
        {"title": "GitLab Runner docs: Docker executor, clearing the Docker cache", "url": "https://docs.gitlab.com/runner/executors/docker/", "type": "docs", "verified": true},
        {"title": "Server Fault search: no space left on device docker overlay2", "url": "https://serverfault.com/search?q=no+space+left+on+device+docker+overlay2", "type": "search", "verified": false},
        {"title": "r/docker search: disk full overlay2", "url": "https://www.reddit.com/r/docker/search/?q=disk+full+overlay2&restrict_sr=1", "type": "search", "verified": false}
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Disk numbers don't add up: deleted-but-open files, inodes, reserved blocks",
      "area": "Disk & resources",
      "level": "L1",
      "tool": "Linux",
      "context": "Two classic puzzles. In the first, inodes run out because of millions of small files. In the second, a deleted file still holds its space because a process keeps it open.",
      "error_text": "No space left on device while df -h shows free space | df shows 100% but du adds up to far less",
      "frequency_signal": "Perennial Server Fault and Stack Overflow questions (\"df vs du\", \"no space left but df shows space\").",
      "root_causes": [
        "Inodes exhausted by session files, mail queues, cache directories or many small image layers",
        "A log deleted with rm while the process keeps writing to the open file handle",
        "logrotate configured without a reload or copytruncate",
        "ext4 reserves 5% of blocks for root, and files can be hidden under a mount point"
      ],
      "diagnosis": [
        "df -i",
        "sudo du --inodes -xd2 / 2>/dev/null | sort -n | tail",
        "sudo lsof -nP +L1",
        "ls -l /proc/PID/fd | grep deleted",
        "sudo tune2fs -l /dev/sdXN | grep -i 'reserved block count'"
      ],
      "fix": [
        "Reload or restart the process that holds the deleted file: sudo systemctl reload UNIT, or restart it",
        "If it can't be restarted: sudo truncate -s 0 /proc/PID/fd/FD",
        "Archive or delete the pile of small files, and fix the application that creates them",
        "Fix logrotate with a postrotate reload or copytruncate"
      ],
      "prevention": "Alert on node_filesystem_files_free as well as on bytes. Never rm a live log: truncate it or let logrotate handle it.",
      "sources": [
        {"title": "Server Fault search: df du difference deleted files lsof", "url": "https://serverfault.com/search?q=df+du+difference+deleted+files+lsof", "type": "search", "verified": false},
        {"title": "Stack Overflow search: no space left on device inodes", "url": "https://stackoverflow.com/search?q=no+space+left+on+device+inodes", "type": "search", "verified": false},
        {"title": "r/linuxadmin search: inodes full", "url": "https://www.reddit.com/r/linuxadmin/search/?q=inodes+full&restrict_sr=1", "type": "search", "verified": false}
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "\"The server is slow\" and no method to find out why",
      "area": "Performance triage",
      "level": "L1-L2",
      "tool": "Linux",
      "context": "This is the vaguest ticket and the most common one. Juniors restart things. Seniors check utilization, saturation and errors for every resource before they touch anything.",
      "error_text": "load average: 24.10, 22.85, 20.02 with a mostly idle CPU | users say \"the site is slow\"",
      "frequency_signal": "Brendan Gregg says the USE method solves about 80% of server issues with 5% of the effort. Slowness is a constant Server Fault topic.",
      "root_causes": [
        "Disk I/O saturation. Tasks in D state count toward the load average.",
        "Memory pressure and swapping",
        "One runaway process, or a noisy neighbour VM that shows up as CPU steal",
        "Network saturation, or DNS timeouts that look like slowness",
        "A slow dependency, such as the database, rather than the host"
      ],
      "diagnosis": [
        "uptime; dmesg -T | tail",
        "vmstat 1 5   # r, b, si/so, wa, st",
        "mpstat -P ALL 1 3; pidstat 1 3",
        "iostat -xz 1 3   # %util and await per device",
        "free -m; sar -n DEV 1 3; sar -n TCP,ETCP 1 3",
        "ps -eo state,pid,cmd | awk '$1 ~ /D/'"
      ],
      "fix": [
        "Name the saturated resource first, then act: limit or reschedule the culprit with nice or ionice, and move backups off-peak",
        "Set container CPU and memory limits so one app can't starve the host",
        "Fix the slow query or endpoint. Add capacity only after you know the bottleneck."
      ],
      "prevention": "Build node_exporter dashboards per USE resource, and practise with stress-ng so you know what normal looks like.",
      "sources": [
        {"title": "Brendan Gregg: The USE Method", "url": "https://www.brendangregg.com/usemethod.html", "type": "blog", "verified": true},
        {"title": "Server Fault search: high load average low cpu iowait", "url": "https://serverfault.com/search?q=high+load+average+low+cpu+iowait", "type": "search", "verified": false},
        {"title": "r/linuxadmin search: server slow troubleshooting", "url": "https://www.reddit.com/r/linuxadmin/search/?q=server+slow+troubleshooting&restrict_sr=1", "type": "search", "verified": false}
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A process was killed without a log line (OOM killer, exit code 137)",
      "area": "Disk & resources",
      "level": "L1",
      "tool": "Linux / Docker / Kubernetes",
      "context": "The app vanishes and its own log ends mid-line, because the kernel or a cgroup memory limit killed it.",
      "error_text": "Out of memory: Killed process 1234 (java) | \"OOMKilled\": true | exit code 137",
      "frequency_signal": "Classic on Stack Overflow and Server Fault. Node OOM and evictions are among the most common themes on k8s.af.",
      "root_causes": [
        "A memory leak or an unbounded cache",
        "No limits are set, so the kernel kills the biggest process, which is often the database",
        "The container limit is lower than the runtime's heap (JVM, Node)",
        "Reading 'used' instead of 'available' in free, so real memory pressure goes unnoticed"
      ],
      "diagnosis": [
        "journalctl -k -b | grep -iE 'out of memory|oom-kill|killed process'",
        "docker inspect -f '{{.State.OOMKilled}} {{.State.ExitCode}}' CONTAINER",
        "kubectl describe pod POD | grep -A5 'Last State'",
        "free -h   # read the 'available' column",
        "ps aux --sort=-rss | head"
      ],
      "fix": [
        "Set limits and requests that match real usage, and size heaps to the limit (for example -XX:MaxRAMPercentage=75)",
        "Fix the leak. Scheduled restarts are toil, not a fix.",
        "For systemd services, set MemoryMax= in a drop-in"
      ],
      "prevention": "Alert when the working set passes 90% of the limit, and on restart counts (kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total).",
      "sources": [
        {"title": "Kubernetes Failure Stories (k8s.af)", "url": "https://k8s.af/", "type": "postmortem", "verified": true},
        {"title": "Stack Overflow search: exit code 137 OOMKilled docker", "url": "https://stackoverflow.com/search?q=exit+code+137+OOMKilled+docker", "type": "search", "verified": false},
        {"title": "Server Fault search: \"Out of memory: Killed process\"", "url": "https://serverfault.com/search?q=%22Out+of+memory%3A+Killed+process%22", "type": "search", "verified": false}
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "It's always DNS: things stop resolving after a change",
      "area": "Network & DNS",
      "level": "L1-L2",
      "tool": "Ubuntu systemd-resolved / Docker embedded DNS / CoreDNS",
      "context": "Name resolution sits under everything, so a DNS change looks like an app, registry or CI outage. AWS's October 2025 us-east-1 outage started when automation left an empty DNS record for DynamoDB's regional endpoint.",
      "error_text": "Temporary failure in name resolution | curl: (6) Could not resolve host | lookup harbor.example.com on 127.0.0.53:53: server misbehaving",
      "frequency_signal": "DNS is one of the two biggest themes on k8s.af, with 8 of 57 stories. AWS's 2025 postmortem is a DNS race. \"It's always DNS\" is a running sysadmin joke.",
      "root_causes": [
        "netplan nameservers or search domains changed or lost",
        "/etc/resolv.conf symlink replaced, or split DNS for an internal zone (Domains=~corp.example) not set in systemd-resolved",
        "A firewall blocks port 53, including TCP, and containers fall back to 8.8.8.8",
        "Negative answers and long TTLs stay cached after a record change",
        "Automation wrote an empty or wrong record"
      ],
      "diagnosis": [
        "resolvectl status; resolvectl query HOST",
        "getent ahosts HOST   # what applications actually see",
        "dig +short HOST @INTERNAL_DNS",
        "ls -l /etc/resolv.conf",
        "sudo tcpdump -ni any port 53",
        "docker exec CONTAINER cat /etc/resolv.conf"
      ],
      "fix": [
        "Fix the nameservers in netplan and apply with sudo netplan try",
        "For containers, set \"dns\": [\"192.0.2.53\"] in /etc/docker/daemon.json and restart Docker",
        "sudo resolvectl flush-caches after the record is fixed",
        "Restore the record from zone history or DNS-as-code"
      ],
      "prevention": "Lower TTLs before planned moves, run two internal resolvers, and probe critical names with blackbox_exporter's DNS module.",
      "sources": [
        {"title": "AWS: Summary of the Amazon DynamoDB service disruption (us-east-1, October 2025)", "url": "https://aws.amazon.com/message/101925/", "type": "postmortem", "verified": true},
        {"title": "Kubernetes Failure Stories (k8s.af)", "url": "https://k8s.af/", "type": "postmortem", "verified": true},
        {"title": "Server Fault search: temporary failure in name resolution systemd-resolved", "url": "https://serverfault.com/search?q=temporary+failure+in+name+resolution+systemd-resolved", "type": "search", "verified": false},
        {"title": "r/sysadmin search: \"always DNS\"", "url": "https://www.reddit.com/r/sysadmin/search/?q=%22always+DNS%22&restrict_sr=1", "type": "search", "verified": false}
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Clock drift breaks TLS, logins, apt and log timelines",
      "area": "Certificates & time",
      "level": "L1",
      "tool": "Linux (systemd-timesyncd / chrony)",
      "context": "On-prem networks often block outbound NTP without offering an internal time server, and restored or paused VMs drift. TLS, TOTP, Kerberos, apt and SIEM timelines then break in confusing ways.",
      "error_text": "x509: certificate has expired or is not yet valid | Release file ... is not valid yet | Clock skew too great",
      "frequency_signal": "Time has its own category in Dan Luu's postmortem collection, with leap-second panics and a leap-year certificate outage.",
      "root_causes": [
        "UDP 123 is blocked and there is no internal NTP server",
        "The VM was restored from a snapshot or paused",
        "Two time daemons are fighting (chrony and systemd-timesyncd)",
        "The hardware clock is in local time on a dual-boot machine"
      ],
      "diagnosis": [
        "timedatectl; timedatectl timesync-status",
        "chronyc tracking; chronyc sources -v",
        "journalctl -u systemd-timesyncd -n 50",
        "for h in host1 host2; do ssh $h date -u; done"
      ],
      "fix": [
        "Point timesyncd at internal servers: set NTP=ntp1.example.internal in /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf, then sudo systemctl restart systemd-timesyncd",
        "Or use chrony with server lines, and run sudo chronyc makestep once for a large offset",
        "Allow UDP 123 to the internal NTP servers"
      ],
      "prevention": "Alert on node_timex_sync_status == 0 or abs(node_timex_offset_seconds) > 0.05.",
      "sources": [
        {"title": "Dan Luu: post-mortems collection (Time section)", "url": "https://github.com/danluu/post-mortems", "type": "postmortem", "verified": true},
        {"title": "Server Fault search: timesyncd not synchronized", "url": "https://serverfault.com/search?q=timesyncd+not+synchronized", "type": "search", "verified": false},
        {"title": "Stack Overflow search: \"certificate has expired or is not yet valid\" clock", "url": "https://stackoverflow.com/search?q=%22certificate+has+expired+or+is+not+yet+valid%22+clock", "type": "search", "verified": false}
      ]
    }
